{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California tenant screening fee cap and refund duties","requirement":"A landlord may not charge a screening fee above the annual cap ($68.96 for 2026) and must give the applicant a receipt, an itemized expense list, a copy of any credit report within 7 days, and a refund of any unused portion.","key_value":"$68.96 (2026)","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental applicants; a screening fee is charged when a rental unit is actually available.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"A screening fee may be charged when a rental unit is actually available; an unselected applicant must be refunded, or applications are reviewed in the order received and the first qualified applicant gets the unit.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026","valid_through":"2026","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"The maximum tenant screening fee for 2026 is\n$68.96\nLandlords may use screening fees to obtain information about a prospective tenant like a consumer credit report.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California reusable tenant screening report requirement","requirement":"A landlord must use a reusable tenant screening report an applicant provides, and must give the applicant a copy of any consumer credit report obtained with the screening fee within 7 days of receiving it.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental applicants who provide a reusable screening report or pay a screening fee.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when an applicant provides a reusable screening report, or when a screening fee is used to obtain a consumer credit report.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"Prospective tenants should expect to receive a copy of their credit report within 7 days of receipt by the landlord, a receipt for the fee paid and an itemized list of any out-of-pocket expenses and time spent by the landlord on the screening, and a refund of any unused portion of the fee. If the tenant provides a reusable screening report, the landlord must use it.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Berkeley tenant screening and application fee rules","requirement":"Before taking a screening fee, an owner must give the applicant a tenant screening fee rights statement and the state fee cap; an owner may not charge an existing tenant a non-refundable fee to renew a tenancy or to change a roommate.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental agreements in Berkeley; owners or agents who charge a fee to obtain a consumer credit report to process a rental application.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to all residential rental agreements regardless of contrary lease terms; the disclosure duty attaches when an owner charges a screening fee.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Any provision of an existing rental agreement or lease that violates the chapter is unenforceable.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.78","quoted_span":"An owner of residential rental property (or the owner’s agent) cannot charge a non-refundable fee to any existing tenant for the purpose of renewing a tenancy, in whole or in part, including any fee associated with the departure of a roommate or to request to add or replace a roommate in a pre-existing household.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
